     "text": "After the failure of the Cripps proposals, a series of meetings and exchange of letters began between the Quaid-e-Azam and Gandhi regarding the political future of India, which is remembered as the Gandhi–Jinnah talks. These talks too could not produce any positive result. After this the Viceroy of India, Lord Wavell, called a conference of the political leaders of India at Simla in June 1945 regarding the political future of India. For participation in the conference a delegation consisting of five Muslim members was proposed, but the Congress insisted that it was the sole representative party of India and that it would also nominate representatives on the seats reserved for Muslims; but the Quaid-e-Azam made it clear that all five Muslim members could be nominated only by the Muslim League, because it alone is the sole party of the Muslims. Besides this, the Unionist Party of the Punjab wanted to appoint a representative of its own on the Muslim seats, whereas this was not acceptable to the Muslim League. At the Simla Conference it could not be decided whether the Muslim League was the sole representative party of the Muslims or not; however, this conference ended without reaching any conclusion.",
