   "content": "<p>Map: Areas that went to India</p><p>The problem of administrative affairs</p><p>About two and a half months passed between Lord Mountbatten’s arrival in India and the announcement of the 3 June Plan. In these few months it was decided to create Pakistan and partition India. According to one estimate this haste was a collusion of the British and the Hindus. They thought that in such circumstances the establishment of Pakistan would prove unstable and thus Pakistan would soon be crushed under various problems. When the plan for the partition of India was announced, the areas that came into Pakistan’s share were less developed and backward, and this newly born state of Pakistan did not even have a suitable capital to run the country.</p><p>To run administrative affairs there was only the city of Karachi, which could bear the burden of running the administrative affairs of a province, but was in no way suitable as the capital of a country. After the creation of Pakistan there were no offices for government employees in the city of Karachi (there was also an extreme shortage of articles of daily office needs). Government employees to run administrative affairs were also scarce. Especially in important administrative posts there was a severe shortage of employees. In fact, in united India almost all important administrative posts were held by non-Muslims/Hindus, who went to India after the establishment of Pakistan. In this way this newly born state of Pakistan had to face a severe shortage of government and non-government employees.</p><p>However, despite these most difficult problems the government employees did not lose heart, and under the inspiring leadership of the Quaid-e-Azam Muhammad Ali Jinnah they faced all the difficulties firmly and gradually overcame these difficulties.</p><p>The problem of refugees</p><p>As the outlines of the plan for the partition of India became clear, a general massacre of Muslims was begun in the Muslim-minority provinces of India under a well-thought-out plan. In these circumstances the Muslims of India had no option except migration towards Pakistan. So refugees in lakhs began coming towards Pakistan. Most of them were people who, apart from the clothes on their bodies, …</p>",
