   "content": "<p>… a restlessness spread among the Muslims. They wanted to win freedom from the clutches of the Dogra Maharaja. However, the Maharaja was not ready for this. So he adopted the path of violence through state officials to suppress the Muslims. The Kashmiri Muslims, who were well aware of the intentions of the Hindu Maharaja, rose up against the Maharaja’s oppression and for the first time began a rebellion at Poonch. With this the conditions of the state deteriorated further. When the Maharaja’s atrocities crossed all limits, the brave tribesmen of the province of Khyber Pakhtunkhwa girded themselves to help their Kashmiri brothers. Before the joint struggle of the tribal and Kashmiri mujahideen the Maharaja’s forces became helpless, and the Maharaja fled from Srinagar to Jammu and appealed to Bharat for military help.</p><p>Before the regular forces of Bharat it became difficult for the untrained Kashmiri mujahideen and tribesmen to fight. The Indian forces began advancing on the liberated parts of Kashmir. In these circumstances, for the protection of Azad Kashmir and the Pakistani borders, orders were issued in 1948 to the armed forces of Pakistan to stop the advance of the Indian forces, and thus the forces of Bharat and Pakistan came face to face for the first time. This war between Bharat and Pakistan was stopped on 1 January 1949 according to a resolution of the United Nations.</p><p>In January 1948 Bharat had presented the Kashmir issue in the Security Council of the United Nations. In the UN Security Council the representatives of both countries presented their positions, after which it was decided that the future of Kashmir would be decided by its inhabitants through a plebiscite. Both countries, Bharat and Pakistan, accepted this decision. But later Bharat began making excuses, and thus to this day the Kashmiris are waiting to decide their future through a plebiscite.</p><p>The Quaid-e-Azam’s vision of Pakistan</p><p>After the establishment of Pakistan, various concepts were found among the political, religious and intellectual circles of the country about this newly born Muslim state. However, the founder of Pakistan stated his vision very clearly on various occasions. The most important and clear words he uttered on 11 August 1947 while addressing the Constituent Assembly of Pakistan. He said:</p><p>“If you work together in a spirit of cooperation, forgetting the past and burying the old quarrels, success will be your destiny. If you change your past and work together with one spirit, then it does not matter at all which group you belong to; you are first, second and last a citizen of this state, where the rights, privileges and responsibilities of all are equal. If work is done with this spirit you can make immense progress. We are going forward with this very spirit and basic principle that we are all equal citizens of this country. Whatever religion, tribe or nation you may belong to, that has nothing to do with the business of the state. My view is that we should keep this very ideal principle in view; in time you will find that the Hindu will no longer remain a Hindu and the Muslim will no longer remain a Muslim — not in the religious sense, because that is the personal matter of every individual, but in the political sense, as citizens of this country.”</p><p>When we reflect on this speech, we learn that these thoughts were expressed on an occasion when the whole subcontinent was in the grip of communal riots. Lakhs of Muslims and Hindus were falling victim to sectarianism. Then the Quaid-e-Azam spoke of communal harmony and urged tolerance and working together. The Quaid-e-Azam also said that religious tolerance and the rights of non-Muslims are exactly in accordance with the spirit of Islam. He further said that its very teachings give us the lesson of democracy and equality, and we shall run the affairs of the state according to these very principles.</p><p>Rehabilitation of refugees</p><p>As a result of partition, about 6.5 million refugees had been compelled by communal riots to take refuge in Pakistan. Solving so large a problem smoothly was a difficult test. Since the largest caravans of refugees were coming towards Lahore, the Quaid-e-Azam himself went there in October 1947 to review the rehabilitation of refugees. On 30 October 1947, addressing a gathering in Lahore, …</p>",
