   "content": "<p>Chapter Three</p><p>The Land of Pakistan</p><p>If we look at the map of the continent of Asia, we shall find that to its south lies a peninsula-like piece of land which is called South Asia. The Himalaya mountains and their branches separate it from the northern part of the continent. The land of Pakistan is spread in the north-western part of South Asia between longitudes 61° and 76° east and latitudes 24° and 37° north. Its total area is 796,096 square kilometres. Together with the Northern Areas and the Western Tribal Areas the total area becomes 868,591 square kilometres.</p><p>To the north of Pakistan lie the mountain ranges of the Himalaya and the Karakoram. Here the Wakhan strip, an area of Afghanistan, separates Pakistan from Tajikistan. To the north-west lies Afghanistan. To the west is the Islamic country of Iran. To the north-east of Pakistan, besides the occupied state of Jammu and Kashmir, lie the areas of the People’s Republic of China, Xinjiang and Tibet. To the east lie the areas of Bharat, Punjab and Rajputana. To the south of Pakistan are the Arabian Sea and the Indian Ocean.</p><p>According to the census of 2017 our population has exceeded 207.7 million.</p><p>Importance of Pakistan’s location</p><p>Pakistan is located, geographically and politically, at the centre of Asia and in one of the most sensitive regions of the world. Pakistan’s location is of great importance for international trade, regional integrity and the interests of the Islamic world. Only a few regions in the world are as important in terms of location.</p><p>Pakistan lies in the middle of the Islamic world, which is spread from Morocco in the west to Indonesia in the east. In the north are the Muslim states of Central Asia and China’s province of Xinjiang. Most of these countries are rich in the wealth of oil. The Muslim countries of South-East Asia, with some mineral and agricultural products, …</p>",
