   "content": "<p>… are important for their mineral and agricultural products. We have historical, religious, cultural and commercial ties with all these countries.</p><p>Map: The Muslim World</p><p>In the north-east Pakistan’s borders meet China. Trade and traffic with China take place by way of the Karakoram Highway. The Karakoram Highway is of great importance commercially and militarily.</p><p>Pakistan – the gateway of Central Asia</p><p>To the south of Pakistan is the Arabian Sea, which is the northern part of the Indian Ocean. From the point of view of international politics it has always remained the centre of special attention. On the shore of the Arabian Sea is Karachi, the most important port, which, being situated on international sea and air routes, plays an important role in the links between European, Asian and African countries. The new port of Gwadar, being built with the help of China, will be able to meet the growing demands of these very links. Because of the facility of these ports the importance of Pakistan has increased further.</p><p>Afghanistan, Tajikistan, Uzbekistan, Kyrgyzstan, Kazakhstan, Turkmenistan and Azerbaijan are surrounded by land. They have no port of their own through which access to international sea-trade routes could easily be obtained. Afghanistan already depends on Pakistan for its foreign trade. After winning independence from the Soviet Union, centuries-old ties between these Muslim states and Pakistan are being restored. Agreements of mutual partnership and cooperation have been made in oil, gas, electricity, tourism and culture, banking, industry and trade, education and several other fields. These Muslim countries and Afghanistan have also been included in the Economic Cooperation Organisation (formerly R.C.D.) consisting of Iran, Turkey and Pakistan. The vast region consisting of these countries, rich in natural resources, will in the near future be blessed with economic progress and prosperity. For this purpose the means of transport have basic importance. So an agreement has been made with Afghanistan to lay a railway line from Quetta to Kandahar. A plan to establish a rail link with the Central Asian Muslim states is under consideration. The region of Central Asia and the Caspian Sea is said to be the area of the world’s largest reserves of oil and gas. An agreement has also been made to lay a gas pipeline from Turkmenistan via Afghanistan.</p>",
