   "content": "<p>Chapter Four</p><p>Steps to make Pakistan an Islamic Republic</p><p>Pakistan came into being on the basis of the “Two-Nation Theory” and the “Ideology of Pakistan”. The basis of the Ideology of Pakistan was laid on the Islamic system of life, by which is meant such a social, political and economic system whose laws, rules and regulations have been framed in the light of Islamic teachings and whose basic sources are the Qur’an and Sunnah. In view of the founding leaders of Pakistan, the basic aim was not merely to obtain a piece of land, but the establishment of an Islamic society on this territory, in which there would be a democratic form of government in the light of the basic principles of Islam, where everyone would have the right to live with complete freedom according to his religious beliefs.</p><p>After the establishment of Pakistan the founder of Pakistan, the Quaid-e-Azam Muhammad Ali Jinnah, did not live long, and thus the concept of Pakistan that was in his mind could not take shape as a concrete reality. The Pakistan the Quaid-e-Azam had in mind was the concept of such an Islamic democratic country whose laws and constitution would be based on the principles of equality, justice and tolerance, but whose source would be the golden principles of Islam.</p><p>The Objectives Resolution</p><p>After the establishment of Pakistan the first Constituent Assembly was established, which was responsible for framing a new constitution. Temporarily, according to the Indian Independence Act, the India Act 1935 was enforced as Pakistan’s interim constitution with amendments as needed, and efforts began for a new constitution. A member of the Constituent Assembly and distinguished scholar, Maulana Shabbir Ahmad Usmani, as a first step towards constitution-making, compiled the draft of a resolution and also obtained the consent of other ulema to it. It was presented in the Constituent Assembly by the late Liaquat Ali Khan on 12 March 1949. In the constitutional history of Pakistan it is …</p>",
