   "content": "<p>5. Reform of the media</p><p>i. Radio and television were used to bring out Islamic and national identity, while some programmes were declared un-Islamic and banned.</p><p>ii. Arrangements were made to teach the Holy Qur’an and the Arabic language through radio and television.</p><p>iii. Programmes based on Islamic teachings were increased, while arrangements were made to show Hajj and other Islamic ceremonies on television.</p><p>After the restoration of the democratic system in the country in 1988, progress towards the enforcement of Islam continued. In May 1991 Parliament approved the Shariat Bill, under which the judicial system of the country will gradually be brought within the bounds of Islamic principles.</p><p>The aim of all these efforts was the establishment of a welfare, democratic and Islamic society in Pakistan. The achievement of this aim is possible only through a continuous and gradual evolutionary process.</p><p>Rights and duties of citizens in the constitution of the Islamic Republic of Pakistan</p><p>In the constitution of Pakistan the rights and duties of citizens have been fixed exactly in accordance with the Islamic system of life.</p><p>Fundamental civil rights in the 1973 constitution</p><p>In the 1973 constitution all the citizens of Pakistan have equal fundamental rights. By fundamental rights are meant those birth rights of man which individuals have in every civilised society. Pakistani society too is a civilised Islamic society; therefore in the constitution of Pakistan the citizens have been guaranteed all fundamental human rights. The aim of including these rights in the constitution is to give them legal shape and to protect them. Of the fundamental rights mentioned in the constitution of Pakistan, a few important rights are as follows.</p><p>i. No person shall be deprived of his right to life and liberty.</p><p>ii. Slavery and forced labour have been prohibited in the constitution.</p><p>iii. Human dignity and the sanctity of the four walls of the home will not be violated.</p><p>iv. All Pakistanis have the right to move about and reside in any part of the country.</p><p>v. The citizens of Pakistan have the right of organisation, forming unions and peaceful assembly.</p><p>vi. Every citizen has the right to obtain lawful employment, trade and business.</p><p>vii. Except government employees, all Pakistanis have the right to take part in politics and obtain membership of any political party.</p><p>viii. Every citizen has been given the right of expression of opinion, and the media, especially the press, will have complete freedom.</p><p>ix. Every citizen has the right to adopt any religion, practise it and preach it.</p><p>x. No citizen shall be arrested without lawful justification. At the time of arrest he shall be informed of the nature of his offence and shall be produced in the court of a magistrate within 24 hours.</p><p>xi. Every Pakistani has the right to acquire, hold, buy and sell land and property in any area of Pakistan, and no one shall be deprived of his property without fulfilling legal requirements.</p><p>xii. No discrimination shall be made against any Pakistani possessing the required qualification on the basis of race, religion, nationality, sex, residence or place of birth in obtaining any government employment.</p><p>xiii. Citizens of every class have the right to preserve and promote their language, script and culture and, according to law, to establish such institutions as may help in achieving this aim.</p>",
