   "content": "<p>… rights, which are included in the United Nations declaration of universal human rights, in some context or other.</p><p>The United Nations Universal Declaration of Human Rights</p><p>On 10 December 1948 the General Assembly of the United Nations approved the Universal Declaration of Human Rights, and all member countries were asked to publicise this declaration and teach it in schools and other educational institutions, so that awareness of basic human rights could be created among people.</p><p>According to this declaration of the United Nations, a few of the rights available to all human beings of the world on the basis of equality are as follows.</p><p>1. All human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights.</p><p>2. All human beings of the world, without distinction of colour, race, nationality or religion, have equal fundamental rights.</p><p>3. Every human being has the right to defend his life, property and liberty.</p><p>4. Slavery and forced labour were prohibited.</p><p>5. No human being shall be subjected to torture or cruel punishment or inhuman treatment.</p><p>6. All human beings are equal before the law and all have equal legal protection.</p><p>7. No one shall be arrested or exiled without justification.</p><p>8. In case of an offence being committed, every person shall be presumed innocent until his offence is proved, and during judicial proceedings every person shall have the full right to defend himself.</p><p>9. Every human being has the right, for any reason, to leave his country and to return to his country.</p><p>10. If not involved in crimes, every human being has the right to take refuge in another country and to obtain protection from persecution.</p><p>11. Every human being has the right to own property individually or collectively, and no one shall be deprived of his ownership of property.</p><p>12. Every human being has the right to adopt any ideology or religion, to hold religious beliefs, to preach and to worship.</p><p>13. Every human being has the right of freedom of expression, peaceful assembly and organisation.</p><p>14. Every person has the right to take part in the system of government of his country directly or through his representatives and to benefit from state services.</p><p>15. Every human being has the right to adopt the occupation of his choice, to obtain equal wages for equal work and to organise (form unions) to protect his professional interests.</p><p>16. All human beings have the right to lead a good and decent standard of life and to have basic facilities for health and welfare, e.g. food, clothing, housing and medical facilities.</p><p>17. All people have the right to acquire education. Basic education will be compulsory and provided free, while opportunities of higher education will be available to all equally on the basis of merit.</p><p>18. Every human being has fundamental rights and freedom, but he will not interfere in the rights and freedom of other human beings.</p><p>Of the basic human rights guaranteed in this United Nations declaration of universal human rights, most are exactly in accordance with the teachings of our religion, Islam.</p>",
