   "content": "<p>Chapter Seven</p><p>Pakistani Languages</p><p>Language is an effective means of communication between human beings. With its help we express our thoughts, feelings and emotions. The present form of language is the result of a long evolutionary process. In the early period of human civilisation various movements, gestures and sounds were used for communicating with one another and making oneself understood. With the passage of time these sounds took the form of words. In the same way, through the mutual connection of these words and their pronunciation in a particular accent, various languages came into existence.</p><p>In various regions of the world thousands of languages are spoken. The early form of all these languages was quite different from their present form, but with the passage of time changes kept occurring in them in view of various needs. Thus the languages of various regions of the world, under the particular conditions of that region, kept adopting a distinct style and tone and accent. In this way a large number of regional languages and dialects were born.</p><p>In the beginning language was limited to speaking only, but later man learnt the skill of bringing words into the form of writing, and thus languages began to be written and read along with being spoken. On reaching the stage of writing, literature was born in various languages. The early form of literature in every language is called “folk literature” (lok adab). In this kind of literature the temperament, cultural background and feelings of the people of a particular area are reflected. After the flourishing of regional and folk literature the period of standard and serious literature began. In this period much was written in the various genres of literature, for example poetry and prose writing, on serious and important subjects.</p><p>Pakistan is situated on such a piece of land where traces of several ancient civilisations have been found. Among these ancient civilisations are included the civilisation of the Indus Valley and the Gandhara civilisation. These civilisations had their own features, social values and cultural characteristics. In the same way, several ancient languages were also connected with them. In Pakistan several languages are spoken, among which, besides the national language Urdu, nearly thirty small and big regional</p>",
