   "content": "<p>Provision of agricultural credit</p><p>Capital is needed to buy various agricultural implements, but the farmers of Pakistan are very poor. For this purpose the Agricultural Development Bank was established, which gives loans to farmers on easy terms.</p><p>Better irrigation</p><p>In Pakistan 67 per cent of cultivation is irrigated by artificial irrigation and the rest by rains. The biggest means of irrigation is canals; in second place are tube wells. In Pakistan water is wasted from canals. A programme has been made for their rehabilitation. To end waterlogging and salinity the SCARP (SCARP) scheme has been made.</p><p>Sale of agricultural produce</p><p>Because of poor roads from field to market, the tactics of commission agents and the shortage of godowns, the farmers of Pakistan face many problems. Accordingly such institutions have been established as buy produce directly from the farmer.</p><p>Increase in the cultivable area</p><p>In Pakistan forty per cent of the cultivable area is lying idle. If it is made fit for cultivation, considerable increase can occur in total agricultural production. For this the government is taking various measures.</p><p>Support price policy</p><p>Under this policy the government itself buys the produce from the farmers at a reasonable price, so that in a year when, because of a bigger crop, the prices of agricultural produce fall, the farmer can be kept safe from that loss.</p><p>Agricultural education and extension</p><p>To promote agricultural education the government has established agricultural universities in Peshawar, Faisalabad, Rawalpindi, Lahore, Karachi and Quetta, where, besides education, research work is also done on agricultural problems.</p><p>Industrial development and the economy</p><p>Pakistan is a backward country industrially and faces these problems:</p><p>1. One reason for Pakistan’s industrial backwardness is that there is a shortage here of such minerals as are necessary for industrial development, for example oil, high-quality coal and iron ore etc.</p><p>2. For Pakistan’s industrial development we have to order machines from foreign countries. Our country’s exports are low, so we do not obtain so much foreign exchange that we can import machines according to need.</p><p>3. Running industries requires a staff with a special kind of training, of which there is a shortage in Pakistan. Therefore industry could not progress much.</p><p>4. Capital is needed for industrial development, but among us there is a shortage of such institutions as can provide loans to industries.</p><p>5. Means of transport too are very necessary for industrial development, so that raw material can be carried to the factories and their products brought to market for sale. But in Pakistan the means of transport are still backward.</p><p>6. Ever since Pakistan was established, political and social conditions have not been calm. The country could not get stability. Therefore industrial development has been affected.</p><p>7. In 1972 the government took many industries, and in 1974 all private commercial banks, into national ownership, because of which industrialists and bankers were discouraged.</p>",
