   "content": "<p>Chapter Eleven</p><p>Problems of Pakistan</p><p>Our country faces many social and economic problems, but we will discuss only a few problems, which are basically connected with the rapidly growing population.</p><p>Population of Pakistan</p><p>The population of Pakistan in 1947 was 33 million, which grew to 151.1 million in 2004–2005; that is, in 58 years the population grew more than fourfold. And now, according to the 2017 census, the population has exceeded 207.77 million. Although the rate of population growth, which was 3.4 per cent in 1991, has decreased to 1.9 per cent, despite this the tendency of increase in population is creating many problems for us. Not only is the number of small children and dependent individuals growing, but unemployment too is increasing indiscriminately. Besides this, pressure too is increasing on the facilities of education and health, housing, agriculture and urban facilities etc. The problems of environmental pollution and of health and sanitation are becoming serious.</p><p>Below are given some basic facts about population growth and national progress:</p><p>Basic facts of population growth and progress</p><p>★ The total area of Pakistan is 796096 square kilometres, which is 0.67 per cent of the total area of the world.</p><p>★ According to the estimates of 2004–2005 the population of Pakistan is 151.1 million, which is 2.36 per cent of the population of the world. At the time of the establishment of Pakistan (1947) this population was 33 million.</p><p>★ At present an annual increase of 2.9 million individuals is taking place in Pakistan’s population, that is, an increase of 8 children every minute.</p><p>★ In 1950 Pakistan was at number 14 in the world in terms of population, whereas in 2004 it is at number 6 (sixth).</p><p>★ In Pakistan the rate of population increase is 1.9 per cent, which at present is more than several other countries of Asia, for example China, India, Sri Lanka and Bangladesh. However, the proportion of child deaths and deaths during childbirth too is much higher than other countries of Asia.</p><p>★ The death rate of newborn children is 83 per thousand live births (Live Births).</p><p>★ The rate of deaths of women during childbirth is 350 per lakh.</p><p>★ In Pakistan the number of men is 52 per cent and the number of women 48 per cent.</p><p>★ In Pakistan per-capita income is 652 dollars. In 1950 Pakistan’s per-capita income was 79 dollars, while South Korea’s per-capita income was 82 dollars. Now South Korea’s per-capita income is 10000 dollars.</p><p>The growing rate of population affects economic progress badly, because the rate of economic progress and the rate of population increase have a close relationship. If the speed of increase in population is greater, then the immediate fulfilment of the needs of the growing population — that is food, clothing, housing, education, health and facilities (electricity, water, gas, telephone) etc. — is not possible. The gap between the demand and supply of the said needs will keep growing. Available resources will keep falling victim to pressure and the standard of living will keep becoming lower. Besides this, to keep the growing population at the present economic standard, investment several times greater will be needed.</p><p>At present the situation of unemployment in the country is alarming, because according to the statistics of 2004, 36 lakh individuals in the country are unemployed. Although the government is taking various measures, for example small schemes have been started, and loans are being given on easy terms for small and medium-scale business. Because of limited employment opportunities in the villages, from the migration of the rural population towards the cities</p>",
