   "content": "<p>Population growth and environmental pollution</p><p>Environmental pollution in today’s age is not the problem of any individual, nation or country, but a global problem. The existence of environmental pollution can be felt everywhere in our universe; it is as if it is less somewhere and more somewhere.</p><p>Although in developed countries there is a great increase in pollution, its prevention too is being done there in a better way. In developing countries or the third world, although less surplus polluting material is being produced, its harmful effects are very great, because no attention at all is being given towards its prevention. Developed countries are badly destroying natural resources to improve their own standard of living, while the countries of the third world are compelled to fulfil the basic needs of their life, for example food, housing, education and health etc.</p><p>Factors producing environmental pollution</p><p>Population growth:</p><p>At the time of the establishment of Pakistan the population of Pakistan was 32.5 million, while in 2004 it became 151.1 million. The rate of population growth in Pakistan is 1.9 per cent, which is higher than most countries of the world.</p><p>The rapid growth of population becomes the cause of severe pressure on national resources, from which many political, economic and social problems are born. For a balanced environment, the balance between natural resources and population according to natural principles is a basic condition. To fulfil the needs of the growing human population — the cutting of forests, the reckless use of chemical fertilisers and insecticides in agriculture, the establishment of factories for the growing needs and facilities of life, and the increase in mechanical means of transport — are becoming the cause of increase in environmental pollution every moment.</p>",
