   "content": "<p>Spread of cities:</p><p>Because of better opportunities of employment, health, education and recreation in cities, many people from villages and towns turn permanently towards cities. Because of natural increase in population and continuous migration from villages to cities, cities spread and the population of cities increases; for example, in the last one and a half hundred years the population of Lahore has increased more than a hundredfold. Through the migration of population many katchi abadis come into existence around cities, in which several kinds of urban facilities, for example roads, electricity, water, gas, and drainage and parks, being absent, filth spreads. Through this disorderly increase of population, pollution is being created.</p><p>Industries:</p><p>At the time of the establishment of Pakistan no industry worth mentioning existed here; our economy depended on agriculture, which was insufficient to fulfil all the needs of the country. To fulfil basic needs, for example food, housing, health and education, besides agriculture, emphasis was laid on setting up as many industries as possible in other sectors.</p><p>In big cities and around them as many industries as possible began to be set up, and this process is still continuing. The aim of these industries was only to obtain as much production as possible, while the harmful effects arising from throwing the poisonous material coming out of them, without cleaning or destroying it, onto water or land were not seriously thought about. While these factories took part in national progress, they also increased pollution, from which very bad effects are being produced on human health.</p><p>Transport:</p><p>To fulfil the travel needs of the growing population, the number of motor vehicles is increasing by more than 2 per cent annually. According to a survey, 70 per cent of motor vehicles in Pakistan have completed their age and emit black smoke harmful to health. Since in Pakistan mostly lead-mixed petrol is used</p>",
